      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-amber-600 mb-2">7,695</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Registrations in the pool (Oct 6, 2026)</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
        If you only looked at BC PNP draws in 2025, you would have seen three Skills Immigration rounds all year, each aimed at very high earners or very high scorers. 2026 looked completely different. After the April 23, 2026 restructuring around three objectives (Care, Build and Innovate), the program settled into a rhythm of roughly two draw days a month: one for targeted Care and Build occupations, and one for "High Economic Impact" candidates under Innovate. This recap pulls every 2026 Skills Immigration draw from the official <a href="https://www.welcomebc.ca/immigrate-to-b-c/invitations-to-apply" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 underline">WelcomeBC invitations page</a> (last updated October 8, 2026) and explains what the numbers show.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-amber-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-amber-900 mb-2">How to read this article</p>
        <p class="text-amber-800">Everything in the tables comes from WelcomeBC. Totals and percentages are our own arithmetic from those tables. Anything about 2027 is analysis based on past patterns, not an official announcement. The BC PNP has not published its 2027 allocation or 2027 priorities.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Phase 1 (February to April 2026): High Economic Impact only</h2>

      <p>
        There were no Skills Immigration draws in January or March 2026. The first three rounds of the year were all "high economic impact" draws, run before the new priorities were announced. Each used two separate tracks: a wage track (hourly <em>and</em> annual minimums plus a NOC TEER 0 to 3 job offer) and a points track.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Date</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Wage track</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Points track</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Total ITAs</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Feb 4, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$70/hr and $145,000/yr: 206</td><td class="px-4 py-3">138+: 223</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">429</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Feb 11, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$62/hr and $125,000/yr: 195</td><td class="px-4 py-3">135+: 265</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">460</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Apr 22, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$62/hr and $125,000/yr: 252</td><td class="px-4 py-3">138+: 232</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">484</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        These three draws alone (1,373 invitations) already exceeded everything the program issued through Skills Immigration in all of 2025. The 2025 archive on WelcomeBC lists only three Skills Immigration rounds that year: May 8 (94 invitations, $105/hr or 150 points), October 2 (474 invitations, $90/hr and $175,000/yr or 140 points) and December 10 (410 invitations, $84/hr and $170,000/yr or 138 points). That is 978 in total. The difference reflects a larger starting allocation in 2026 (5,254 versus 4,000), which you can read about in our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-2026-allocation-priorities" class="text-blue-600 underline">2026 allocation guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Phase 2 (May 2026 onward): targeted Care and Build draws</h2>

      <p>
        From May 6, 2026 the program began separate draws for priority occupations. Each Care or Build draw restricts the pool to people whose registration matches a priority occupation (and any extra condition, such as an ECE certificate or a SkilledTradesBC credential), then invites the highest scorers in that group. The full list of priority occupations is on WelcomeBC's <a href="https://www.welcomebc.ca/immigrate-to-b-c/about-the-bc-provincial-nominee-program/about-the-bc-provincial-nominee-program" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 underline">selection of workers page</a>; we break it down in our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-care-healthcare-occupations-list-2027" class="text-blue-600 underline">Care occupations guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Care and Build draws: minimum score (invitations)</h3>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Date</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Childcare (ECEs)</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Health</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Veterinary</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Construction trades</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">May 6</td><td class="px-4 py-3">115 (86)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">108 (117)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">100 (9)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">108 (121)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Jun 2</td><td class="px-4 py-3">111 (91)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">100 (117)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">92 (6)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">101 (128)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Jul 9</td><td class="px-4 py-3">108 (91)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">96 (116)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">88 (&lt;5)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">97 (136)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Aug 6</td><td class="px-4 py-3">102 (183)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">84 (124)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">72 (6)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">88 (187)</td></tr>
