            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Sep 24</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$52/hr and $105,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">426</td><td class="px-4 py-3">131</td><td class="px-4 py-3">288</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Two details matter here. First, the wage track needs <em>both</em> numbers. Someone earning $52 an hour on a 37.5-hour week makes about $101,400 a year, which would miss the $105,000 annual minimum used on September 24. Second, the points cutoff barely moved (138 in February, 131 in September) while the wage cutoff fell by $18 an hour. The pool is thin at the top: on October 6, only 203 registrations scored 130 or more. Each points round therefore reaches only a few points deeper. For a closer look at this route, see our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-innovate-high-economic-impact-draws-2027" class="text-blue-600 underline">Innovate High Economic Impact guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">2026 by the numbers</h2>

      <p>
        Adding every published Skills Immigration figure from February 4 to October 8 gives the totals below. Four rows were reported only as "&lt;5", so the real totals are slightly higher.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
