            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Care: Health</td><td class="px-4 py-3">699</td><td class="px-4 py-3">about 11%</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Rural/Remote Health Support Initiative</td><td class="px-4 py-3">93</td><td class="px-4 py-3">about 1.5%</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Care: Veterinary (plus three &lt;5 rounds)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">21+</td><td class="px-4 py-3">under 1%</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Care: Education</td><td class="px-4 py-3">&lt;5</td><td class="px-4 py-3">under 0.1%</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-slate-50 font-semibold"><td class="px-4 py-3">Total</td><td class="px-4 py-3">6,297+</td><td class="px-4 py-3">100%</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Two cautions before you draw conclusions. Invitations are not nominations: some invited people never apply, and some applications are refused. And the table leaves out the <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-health-authority-stream" class="text-blue-600 underline">Health Authority stream</a>, where eligible applicants apply directly without registering or receiving an invitation, as well as Entrepreneur Immigration. The table measures where <em>invitations</em> went, not the full use of the 6,254 nominations. Still, the split is clear. Roughly 63% of 2026 Skills Immigration invitations went through the Innovate tracks, which have no occupation list. Targeted Care and Build draws made up most of the rest.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The pool on October 6, 2026</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Score range</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Registrations</th>
