      <p>
        Most registrants (about 71%) score below 110. The 131-point Innovate cutoff is out of reach for them, but a sub-100 score can still win an invitation if the person is in a priority Care or Build occupation. That is the single most important fact about the 2026 system. Your occupation and credentials now decide which contest you are in, and your score decides your rank within it. You can check where you would land with our <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP points calculator</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Seven lessons from 2026 for 2027 applicants</h2>

      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-4">
        <li><strong>Expect a slow first quarter.</strong> In 2025 the first Skills Immigration draw came on May 8. In 2026 it came on February 4, with nothing in January or March. Both years, BC was waiting to confirm its federal allocation and set priorities. If that pattern holds, early 2027 may be quiet too.</li>
        <li><strong>Credentials are the gate, not a bonus.</strong> Targeted draws invite only people who meet the condition, such as an ECE One Year or Five Year certificate, Care Aide Registry registration for NOC 33102, or a SkilledTradesBC certificate or registered apprenticeship. Under section 7.3 of the Skills Immigration Program Guide, if you are invited on a factor you did not actually meet, your application will be refused.</li>
        <li><strong>Thresholds eased as the year went on.</strong> Cutoffs and wage minimums drifted down through 2026, and the largest rounds (August 20: 602; September 24: 714) came right after the August 18 increase of 1,000 nominations. Applicants who registered early and kept their registration accurate benefited.</li>
        <li><strong>Mind the 12-month clock.</strong> Under the program guide, a registration that is not invited within 12 months is removed from the pool automatically. A registration filed in late 2026 could expire before the 2027 rhythm settles. Note the date and plan to re-register if needed.</li>
        <li><strong>The wage track is a two-part test.</strong> Hourly and annual minimums both apply, and bonuses, overtime and allowances do not count toward the hourly wage in the program guide.</li>
        <li><strong>Regional work matters twice.</strong> A job outside Metro Vancouver earns up to 25 area points (15 for Area 3, 5 for Area 2, plus 10 for regional experience or regional alumni), and the program aims for at least 35% of nominations outside Metro Vancouver. See our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-look-west-regional-nominations-2027" class="text-blue-600 underline">Look West regional guide</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Thirty days, no extensions.</strong> After an invitation you have 30 calendar days to submit a complete application, and that period cannot be extended. Prepare documents before you are invited; our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-documents-checklist" class="text-blue-600 underline">documents checklist</a> helps.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">2027 forecast: what we expect (analysis, not official)</h2>

      <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-blue-900 mb-2">Status as of October 14, 2026</p>
        <p class="text-blue-800">BC has not received or announced a 2027 allocation. The federal 2027–2029 Immigration Levels Plan has not been tabled yet. Everything in this section is our reading of 2025 and 2026 patterns.</p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Draw structure</h3>
      <p>
        We expect the Care, Build and Innovate framework to continue into 2027. It is written into the current program guide and the selection of workers page, and BC tied it to its Look West economic strategy. Nothing official says it will end on December 31. The program guide also says the BC PNP can change selection factors, scoring and draw frequency at any time. A change in the priority lists (for example, adding or removing an occupation) is more likely than a return to general points-only draws.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Cutoff direction</h3>
      <p>
        If early 2027 has a lull like early 2025 and 2026, registrations will build up in the pool. When draws restart, we expect first-round cutoffs in each category to open <em>higher</em> than the autumn 2026 levels, then ease as the year goes on. That is what happened in 2026. How far they fall will depend mostly on the size of BC's 2027 allocation. We are not publishing predicted cutoff numbers. A single policy decision, such as a new priority occupation or a mid-year allocation increase, can move a category by 10 to 20 points in one round, as Health and Veterinary did in 2026.
      </p>

